n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Reversal: adopt exports:true after all\">\n**Reversing my previous decision — restore the `exports: true` work.** The owner has clarified two things that invalidate the premise I told you to reject on:\n\n1. The `0.0.1-alpha.1` npm publish was purely a **name reservation** — there are no consumers, so \"silent contract change on a published package\" is a non-concern. That premise was carrying the entire rejection.\n2. **ESM-only is the explicit intent** for this package.\n\nWith that corrected, re-read your own findings: bare-string subpath exports are the *correct* shape for a pure-ESM package (TypeScript resolves `dist/*.d.ts` as siblings implicitly, and **attw's esm-only profile passing clean is authoritative** on exactly that question — it's the tool whose entire job is verifying type resolution across module modes). And `./package.json` is additive and commonly recommended so tooling can read the manifest. Neither is a defect; I was optimizing for contract-preservation that doesn't apply here.\n\nSo: un-stash / re-apply `exports: true`, and verify the one genuinely open issue empirically — the tracked-file mutation vs. `git diff --exit-code` in the `package` job. Expected resolution: commit the tsdown-generated `package.json`, after which a build regenerates identical bytes and the diff check passes. Confirm that's actually true by running build twice and checking the tree stays clean. If it holds, it's better than neutral — it becomes a drift gate: editing `entry` without rebuilding+committing will fail CI, which is what you want.\n\nIf instead the generated output is non-deterministic across builds (key ordering, formatting churn vs. prettier), report that — that WOULD be a genuine blocker, and different from the one I wrongly rejected on.\n\nRewrite the deviation note accordingly: `exports: true` **adopted** on 2026-07-27, deriving the `exports` map from tsdown's `entry` list; bare-string ESM subpaths