ment, connection-failure diagnostics, cancellation, simulated hangs. That's **addon logic**. None of it varies by installation method or Storybook version. It's already covered by unit tests and by the workspace fixture's existing `visual` job, and that's its correct permanent home.\n\nWhat Task 8 must prove is narrower and different in kind: **the packed tarball, installed into a real non-workspace project, works.** Dev server boots with the addon loaded from `node_modules`; it registers in the testing widget; a story captures; a changed story shows a diff; approval writes the baseline; rerun passes; static build reports unavailable. That's the core loop, and it's exactly what an installation and a Storybook-version bump can break.\n\nSo:\n\n- **Split `test/acceptance/addon-suite.ts` by what it requires**, not by depth. A **core suite** that needs only \"a Storybook with at least one deterministic capturable story\" (the loop above), and a **scenario suite** that needs the elaborate fixture plumbing. This isn't smoke/full tiering for speed — it's organizing tests by their actual preconditions, which is why my earlier \"no tiering\" instruction doesn't apply. The scenario suite keeps running against `test/fixtures/project` in the existing `visual` job, unchanged.\n- **Examples stay genuinely simple** human quickstarts, as briefed. No overlay, no temp fixture copies, no second mode in `fixture-server.ts` — that machinery disappears entirely once the full suite isn't the target.\n- **Standardize the demo story IDs across all three examples** (same demo component, same story ids) so the core suite can target them directly without per-example parameterization. Include at least one story whose rendering you can deterministically change to exercise the diff/approve path.\n- Consumer cells run the core suite only.\n\nThis keeps examples honest as quickstarts, keeps the scenario coverage where it belongs, and drops the machinery. If the core/scenario split turns out to be messier in practice than it sounds — e.g. the two share substantial helper code — share the helpers, split only the specs, and report anything that resists.\n\nCarry on; everything else in the brief is unchanged.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Final: adopt exports:true; examples carry scenarios\">\nTwo final decisions — these supersede everything I've said on both topics. Sorry for the churn; both are now anchored to evidence rather than my assumptions.\n\n## 1. exports — ADOPT `exports: true` (final, and this time it holds)\n\nThe owner asked the right question: do we actually need the explicit `types` condition, or does it resolve by default? Answer: **it resolves by default.** For a pure-ESM package, TS resolves through the exports map to `dist/index.js` and finds `dist/index.d.ts` as a sibling — which tsdown emits and the pack-inventory test already asserts ships. **Your own attw esm-only run passing clean is the proof**, and attw is the authority on exactly this question. The explicit condition only matters for `moduleResolution: node` consumers, who can't read exports maps at all — so it buys nothing for our target set.\n\nSo the earlier \"keep types\" constraint is resolved by there being nothing to keep: adopt `exports: true`, drop the hand-authored map. It's one config line derived from `entry` instead of ~20 hand-synced lines. The added `./package.json` export is fine (additive, commonly recommended).\n\nStill verify the one genuinely open thing: **byte-stability across builds.** Run build twice, confirm the tree stays clean, commit the generated manifest. If the output churns (key ordering, prettier disagreement), that IS a blocker — report it and fall back to hand-authored. Deviation note: adopted 2026-07-27, bare-string ESM subpaths are correct for an ESM-only package and attw-verified, committed generated manifest doubles as a drift gate.\n\n## 2. The fork — option A: examples carry the scenarios (reverses my last message)\n\nThe owner's reasoning, which is better than mine: for a visual-testing tool, the edge cases *are* the product. A developer cloning an example and finding a stale approval, a malformed baseline, a story outside `storyRoots`, disabled capture, viewport framing, and portal capture learns what the addon actually does. Two demo buttons teach nothing. Documentation-by-example.\n\nSo: **examples carry the full scenario story set**, not a minimal demo. No overlay, no temp copies, no second mode in `fixture-server.ts`, no core/scenario spec split. CI runs `storybook dev` in the example directory unmodified and points Playwright at it. Simplest wiring — your option A.\n\nTwo refinements:\n\n**(a) Fault-injection plumbing stays in the workspace fixture.** There's a line inside those scenarios. Demonstrable behaviors — changed pixels, disabled stories, viewport vs content framing, portal capture, out-of-`storyRoots` confinement, malformed baseline metadata, stale approval — are real things a user hits, and belong in the examples. But the `control/state.json`-driven simulated hang and forced connection failure are **fault injection**: harness machinery that would be nonsense in a demo project (\"here's a story that deliberately breaks the capture\"). Keep those specs and their control plumbing in `test/fixtures/project`, run by the existing `visual` job. Everything else moves to / is mirrored in the examples.\n\n**(b) Organize the stories so they read as documentation.** Group them (e.g. a `Basics` section first, then `Scenarios`), and give each scenario story a short description saying what it demonstrates and what the expected addon behavior is. That's what converts test scaffolding into a good example. The example READMEs should point at that structure.\n\nWorth flagging in your PR body as a follow-up (do NOT do it in this PR): once the examples carry every demonstrable scenario, `test/fixtures/project` shrinks to fault-injection-only, and a later change could delete most of that parallel tree. Real simplification, but too much for one PR.\n\nEverything else in the original brief stands.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Durable context to record in repo docs\">\nConsolidating the standing context behind tonight's decisions. Two of these you should **write into the repo**, not just act on — they've been re-litigated several times in one session (by me, mostly), and they should be settled for future contributors and agents.\n\n## 1. The published npm version is a name reservation, not a contract\n\n`storyproof@0.0.1-alpha.1` exists **solely to claim the npm name**. It has zero consumers, is published under the `alpha` dist-tag only, and its README says outright it isn't a usable release. **Never let \"but it's already published\" gate a decision** — that premise is what made me wrongly reject `exports: true` the first time. Breaking changes are free until `0.1.0` has real users; that's what the prerelease sequence in the release plan exists to manage.\n\nRecord this in the release plan near the Task 9 metadata section (it's already noted there as a deviation; make the *implication* explicit, not just the fact).\n\n## 2. ESM-only is permanent intent, not an accident\n\nNo CJS build, ever, unless a demonstrated consumer requires it. This is why bare-string subpath exports are correct, why attw runs the `esm-only` profile, and why the `types` condition is redundant. Record in root AGENTS.md alongside the toolchain description so nobody \"helpfully\" adds a CJS output later.\n\n## 3. Examples are documentation-by-example — that's the acceptance bar\n\nThe examples exist to *teach the addon by showing it*, and their being CI fixtures is a happy consequence, not their purpose. The standard for what goes in: **if a real user will hit it, demonstrate it** — changed pixels, disabled capture, viewport vs content framing, portal capture, out-of-`storyRoots` confinement, malformed baseline metadata, stale approval. Each with a description saying what it demonstrates and what the addon should do. Fault injection (control-file-driven hangs, forced connection failures) is the one exclusion — that's harness machinery, stays in `test/fixtures/project`.\n\nRecord this philosophy in root AGENTS.md next to the `examples/` layout description, phrased as the bar for future examples: a new example or scenario story earns its place by teaching something a user would encounter.\n\nNothing here changes the decisions in my previous message — this is the reasoning behind them, made durable. Carry on.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Examples use workspace:*; CI copies out for tarball test\">\nCorrection to the examples' dependency wiring — my brief had this wrong and it's worth fixing before you wire CI.\n\n## Examples consume the LOCAL package, not the published one\n\nI specified that examples depend on `storyproof@0.0.1-alpha.1` with CI overlaying the tarball. That's unconventional and misleading: a contributor clones the repo, runs an example, and sees an **old published build instead of their own working tree**. Every comp we researched does the opposite — loki's `examples/react` is a workspace member consuming local code, vite forces `workspace:*` on all playgrounds, msw-storybook-addon builds locally.\n\nSo: **make `examples/*` pnpm workspace members** (add `examples/*` to `pnpm-workspace.yaml`) and have each depend on `\"storyproof\": \"workspace:*\"`. A contributor runs `pnpm install` at the root, `cd examples/react-vite-sb10.5`, `pnpm storybook`, and sees their local changes. That's the dev loop the examples should serve.\n\nExpect the root lockfile to grow (three Storybook minors resolved side by side) — that's normal and fine for pnpm's isolated layout.\n\n## CI's consumer job copies OUT of the workspace to test the tarball\n\n`workspace:*` proves nothing about the packed artifact, which is the entire point of Task 8. So the consumer job breaks out of the workspace explicitly — which also gives you the isolation guarantee for free, replacing the negative-control fixture idea:\n\nRoughly (adapt as needed, use `working-directory:`, no bespoke script):\n```\n- copy examples/${{ matrix.example }} to a temp dir outside the repo\n- pnpm pkg set dependencies.storyproof=file:<absolute path to downloaded tarball>\n- pnpm install --ignore-workspace\n- assert the resolved storyproof path is under the temp project's node_modules\n- playwright install --with-deps chromium\n- run the acceptance suite against that copy\n```\n\n`pnpm pkg set` is a first-class pnpm command (same family as `npm pkg set`) — no script, no sed, no JSON surgery. The copy is what makes the test meaningful: outside the workspace, no possible link back to source, real install of the real tarball.\n\nThis is loki's exact two-tier pattern — workspace-linked example for the dev loop, out-of-tree registry/tarball install for the published-artifact proof. Both, for different questions.\n\nEverything else stands: examples carry the full scenario story set with descriptions, fault injection stays in `test/fixtures/project`, `exports: true` adopted pending byte-stability, one Node version for the consumer matrix, version-suffixed example directory names.\n\nWhen you record the examples philosophy in root AGENTS.md, include this distinction explicitly — that examples link the workspace copy for development, and CI proves the packed artifact separately by copying out of the tree. It's the kind of thing that looks redundant until someone \"simplifies\" one of the two away.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Keep the simple demos; confirm you got two corrections\">\n**Keep the Button/NavLink demo stories** — don't make the examples 1:1 with the fixture set. That's exactly the right instinct and matches the standard: a first-time cloner should land on something plainly readable before meeting stale-approval and malformed-baseline scenarios. Organize them so that's the reading order: a `Basics` (or similar) group first with the simple components, then a `Scenarios` group with the fixture-derived stories, each carrying a short description of what it demonstrates and what the addon should do. The example README points at that structure.\n\nTwo messages landed after yours — confirm you've picked both up, because one changes work you may be doing right now:\n\n1. **Examples must depend on `\"storyproof\": \"workspace:*\"`, not the published `0.0.1-alpha.1`.** Add `examples/*` to `pnpm-workspace.yaml`. A contributor should see their own working tree when they run an example, not an old published build. The packed-tarball proof happens in CI by *copying the example out of the repo*, `pnpm pkg set dependencies.storyproof=file:<tarball>`, then `pnpm install --ignore-workspace` — being outside the tree is what makes the isolation real, and it replaces the negative-control fixture idea. (loki's two-tier pattern: workspace link for the dev loop, out-of-tree install for the artifact proof.)\n\n2. **`exports: true` is adopted** (final), and there's durable context to record in the repo — the published alpha is a name reservation with no consumers and must never gate a decision; ESM-only is permanent intent; the examples-as-documentation bar goes in root AGENTS.md.\n\nIf you'd already built examples pinned to the published version, rewiring to `workspace:*` is the fix — flag it rather than working around it.\n</teammate-message>",
