            "content": "## Summary\n\n- Release plan Task 8: a packed-consumer harness proving `storyproof` works as a real installed npm package, not just workspace source.\n- Three Storybook example projects under `examples/` (`react-vite-sb10.5`, `react-vite-sb10.0`, `nextjs-vite-sb10.5`) are real pnpm workspace members (`storyproof: workspace:*`) for local development, and separately serve as documentation-by-example plus CI's acceptance fixture.\n- A new CI `consumer` job proves the actual packed tarball by copying an example **out** of the workspace, installing the exact archive the `package` job built there, and running the existing reusable acceptance suite against it.\n- Adopted tsdown's `exports: true` to derive `package.json`'s `exports` map from its `entry` list instead of hand-syncing both.\n- No new orchestrator scripts anywhere in this PR.\n\nThis PR went through several reversed intermediate decisions during review (recorded in full in the release plan's dated deviation notes, not just the end state) — summarizing the *final* design below.\n\n## `exports: true` — adopted\n\n`tsdown.config.ts` now sets `exports: true`. The generated shape is bare-string subpath targets (`\".\": \"./dist/index.js\"`, no explicit `\"types\"` condition) plus an added `\"./package.json\"` export. This is correct for this permanently-ESM-only package: TypeScript resolves the sibling `dist/*.d.ts` implicitly, and attw's `esm-only` profile (already a build-time gate) verifies exactly that resolution holds with zero problems. Verified **byte-stable** across repeated builds — `pnpm --filter storyproof build` run twice reproduces `package.json` identically — so the committed generated manifest now also functions as a drift gate in the `package` job's existing `git diff --exit-code` check: editing `entry` without rebuilding and committing fails CI.\n\n`test/build-contract.test.ts` was updated to assert the real generated shape directly, while staying independent of `tsdown.config.ts`'s `entry` list itself (deriving a test's expectation from the config it validates would pass vacuously — same principle `test/pack-inventory.test.ts` already follows for the `files` allowlist).\n\nThe full back-and-forth (rejected → adopted → rejected → adopted) and why is recorded as a dated deviation note in the release plan next to Task 6, including the specific correction that mattered: `storyproof@0.0.1-alpha.1` is a name-reservation placeholder with zero consumers, so \"it's already published\" cannot gate a resolution-contract decision on its own.\n\n## Examples: two-tier design\n\n**Workspace-linked for development.** `examples/*` is now in `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, and each example depends on `storyproof: workspace:*`. A contributor runs `pnpm install` at the root, `cd`s into an example, and `pnpm storybook` shows their own working tree, not a stale published build.\n\n**Copied out of the workspace to prove the packed artifact.** A `workspace:*` link proves nothing about the actual npm tarball, which is Task 8's subject. The `consumer` CI job:\n1. Copies an example directory out of the workspace (`cp -r`, *before* any `pnpm install` has populated `node_modules` anywhere in the checkout, so the copy carries none — this is also the isolation guarantee, replacing a separate negative-control fixture).\n2. Points the copy at the exact tarball the `package` job built: `pnpm pkg set dependencies.storyproof=file:<tarball>` (first-class pnpm command, no script).\n3. Installs it there with `pnpm install --ignore-workspace`.\n4. Asserts `import.meta.resolve('storyproof')` resolves under the copy's own `node_modules`.\n5. Runs the reusable acceptance suite (`test/acceptance/addon-suite.ts`) against that copy's real dev server via `test:visual`'s new `VISUAL_TEST_PROJECT_DIR` env var (renamed from an earlier `VISUAL_TEST_CONSUMER_DIR` iteration to match — same mechanism, now pointed at a `mktemp`'d copy instead of an in-repo example).\n\n**`--ignore-workspace` requir