consumer binds to `storyproof/command`. Its `llame`/`@workspace` source scan was a rebrand-migration guard, obsolete since the extraction.\n\n**Do not replace either with anything** — no snapshot, no substitute assertion. And keep the explanatory comment you wrote about not deriving a test's expectations from the config it validates; that principle is right and still applies to `pack-inventory.test.ts`, which stays.\n\nIf any other test asserts configuration rather than behavior, list it in your report rather than deleting it.\n\nFrame it in the CHANGELOG as: config-assertion tests removed; packaging is verified by publint, attw, the pack inventory, and the packed-consumer matrix.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"All 3 consumer cells fail: addon panel never registers\">\nCI on `b29ae82`: 8/8 pre-existing jobs green, **all 3 consumer cells failed identically**. Diagnosis so far, to save you the log archaeology:\n\n- Every failure is a 60s timeout inside `showVisualPanel` → `openVisualPanel` (addon-suite.ts:673/627), plus one `toBeVisible` \"element(s) not found\" on the static-build test. So Storybook **starts and serves** (no connection errors), but the **Visual tests panel never registers**.\n- Same commit's `visual smoke` job passes against the workspace fixture. So the delta is precisely the packed-tarball install path, not the suite, not the stories, not the addon logic.\n\nThat narrows it to: **the addon's manager entry isn't being loaded/bundled when the package is installed from the tarball.** The server side may well be fine (the preset's `experimental_serverChannel`/`experimental_devServer` could be running) — it's the manager UI half that's missing.\n\nStrongest hypothesis, and it connects to a finding from the ecosystem research earlier: **pnpm's symlinked layout.** `node_modules/storyproof` is a symlink into `node_modules/.pnpm/storyproof@file+.../node_modules/storyproof`, so the manager entry's realpath resolves *outside* the project's direct `node_modules`. Storybook's manager builder (esbuild) and Vite both have constraints about serving/bundling files outside the project root — `server.fs.allow`, `optimizeDeps`, and friends. Notably, storycap documented exactly this class of problem: *\"Because of Storybook addon channel restriction, storycap addon code should be put under each Storybook project's own node_modules directory\"* — which is why they hand-inject built output instead of installing normally.\n\nThings worth trying, roughly in order of \"closest to what a real consumer does\":\n1. Check the Storybook dev-server output in the failing job for preset/manager build errors — the webServer stdout should say whether the manager entry resolved. If `fixture-server.ts` swallows that output, surface it; you'll be flying blind otherwise.\n2. Try the consumer install with **npm** rather than pnpm (flat `node_modules`, no symlink indirection). Many real consumers use npm, so this is legitimate, not a workaround — and if it passes, it confirms the symlink hypothesis immediately.\n3. If you want to keep pnpm, try `--config.node-linker=hoisted` (or a `.npmrc` in the copied project) to get a flat layout.\n4. Verify what `import.meta.resolve('storyproof/manager')` actually yields inside the copied project, and whether that path is under the project root after `realpath`.\n\nNote this is a **genuinely valuable finding either way** — it's exactly the class of breakage that only a real packed install can reveal, and that a `workspace:*` link can never show. If the resolution is \"consumers on pnpm need X\", that's documentation the addon owes its users, not just a CI fix. Capture whatever you learn in the release plan.\n\nAlso still outstanding from my earlier message: **delete `test/build-contract.test.ts` and `test/identifier-contract.test.ts` entirely** (both still present at `b29ae82`). Config-assertion tests, verified behaviorally now by publint/attw/pack-inventory/the cons