          "input": {
            "content": "## Summary\n\n- Release plan Task 8: a packed-consumer harness proving `storyproof` works as a real installed npm package, not just workspace source.\n- Three Storybook example projects under `examples/` (`react-vite-sb10.5`, `react-vite-sb10.0`, `nextjs-vite-sb10.5`) are real pnpm workspace members (`storyproof: workspace:*`) for local development, and separately serve as documentation-by-example plus CI's acceptance fixture.\n- A new CI `consumer` job proves the actual packed tarball by copying an example **out** of the workspace, installing the exact archive the `package` job built there, and running the existing reusable acceptance suite against it.\n- Adopted tsdown's `exports: true` to derive `package.json`'s `exports` map from its `entry` list instead of hand-syncing both. Widened `peerDependencies.storybook` to `^10.0.0` to match what's actually tested. Deleted two change-detector tests.\n- **Found and fixed a real product defect**: the Visual tests panel has likely never rendered for any real installed consumer of this package (see below) — caught only because this harness loads the manager UI in an actual browser.\n- No new orchestrator scripts anywhere in this PR.\n\nThis PR went through several reversed intermediate decisions during review (recorded in full in the release plan's dated deviation notes, not just the end state) — summarizing the *final* design below.\n\n## The defect this harness caught\n\nThe first real `consumer` CI run failed on all three matrix cells: every scenario timed out waiting for the \"Visual tests\" tab to appear. I can't run Playwright locally (missing browser system libraries on this dev host), so I reproduced the same install locally and used Chrome browser automation (not Playwright) to open the manager UI directly and read the console:\n\n```\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks')\n    at exports.jsx (.../sb-addons/storyproof-1/manager-bundle.js:314:40)\n    at render (.../sb-addons/storyproof-1/manager-bundle.js:833:48)\n```\n\nRoot cause: `src/manager.tsx` uses the automatic JSX transform, so `dist/manager.js` imports `{ jsx, jsxs }` from `\"react/jsx-runtime\"`. Storybook's manager builder aliases bare `\"react\"`/`\"react-dom\"` imports to its own shared React instance, but not the `jsx-runtime` subpath — so a fresh copy of React's jsx-runtime gets bundled into the addon's manager bundle, one whose `ReactSharedInternals` (confirmed via React's own source: `recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks` is only initialized by `react.development.js`) was never populated by the same React build. Crashes the instant Storybook renders the panel.\n\nI reproduced this identically via `workspace:*`, a packed npm tarball installed with pnpm, and the same tarball installed with npm — ruling out pnpm's symlinked store as the cause (an early hypothesis). It's absent **only** when Storybook compiles `src/manager.tsx` directly from source, which is the only path any test or CI job exercised before this PR — meaning **this is likely a defect that predates Task 8 entirely** and would affect every real consumer today.\n\nFix: `tsconfig.build.json` sets `\"jsx\": \"react\"` (classic transform), scoped to the build surface only (the root tsconfig stays on automatic, since `*.stories.tsx` fixtures don't import `React` themselves and would fail typecheck otherwise). `dist/manager.js` now routes JSX through the already-imported `React` global instead of `react/jsx-runtime`, so there's no jsx-runtime import for Storybook's manager builder to duplicate. Verified fixed by loading the manager UI before/after the change, against both `react-vite` and `nextjs-vite` example projects.\n\n## `exports: true` — adopted\n\n`tsdown.config.ts` now sets `exports: true`. The generated shape is bare-string subpath targets (`\".\": \"./dist/index.js\"`, no explicit `\"types\"` condition) plus an added `\"./package.json\"` export. Correct for this permanently-ESM-only package: TypeScript resolves the sibling `dist/*.d.ts` implicitly, and attw's `esm-only` profile (a build-time gate) verifies exactly that resolution holds with zero problems. Verified **byte-stable** across repeated builds, so the committed generated manifest now also functions as a drift gate in the `package` job's `git diff --exit-code` check.\n\nThe full back-and-forth on this decision (rejected → adopted → rejected → adopted) is recorded as a dated deviation note in the release plan next to Task 6, including the correction that mattered: `storyproof@0.0.1-alpha.1` is a name-reservation placeholder with zero consumers, so \"it's already published\" can't gate a resolution-contract decision on its own.\n\n## Examples: two-tier design\n\n**Workspace-linked for development.** `examples/*` is in `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, each depending on `storyproof: workspace:*`. `pnpm install` at the root, `cd` into an example, `pnpm storybook` — shows a contributor's own working tree, not a stale published build.\n\n**Copied out of the workspace to prove the packed artifact.** A `workspace:*` link proves nothing about the actual npm tarball, which is Task 8's subject. The `consumer` CI job:\n1. Copies an example directory out of the workspace (`cp -r`, *before* any `pnpm install` has populated `node_modules` anywhere in the checkout — this doubles as the isolation guarantee, replacing a separate negative-control fixture).\n2. Points the copy at the exact tarball the `package` job built: `pnpm pkg set dependencies.storyproof=file:<tarball>` (first-class pnpm command, no script).\n3. Installs it there with `pnpm install --ignore-workspace`.\n4. Asserts `import.meta.resolve('storyproof')` resolves under the copy's own `node_modules`.\n5. Runs the reusable acceptance suite (`test/acceptance/addon-suite.ts`) against that copy's real dev server via `test:visual`'s new `VISUAL_TEST_PROJECT_DIR` env var.\n\n**`--ignore-workspace` requirement, confirmed empirically**: a plain `pnpm install` inside a directory under an ancestor `pnpm-workspace.yaml` silently no-ops (reports \"Done\" without installing anything), and a plain `pnpm add` there rewrites the *root* lockfile instead of the target's own.\n\n## Examples are documentation-by-example, minus fault injection\n\nEach example carries a plain demo (`Button` / `NavLink`) **and** the same `visual-fixture`/`outside-fixture` scenario stories as `test/fixtures/project` — changed pixels, a disabled story, exact viewport framing, portal capture, a story outside `storyRoots`, stale-approval rejection, malformed baseline metadata — each with a short code comment on what it demonstrates. `storySort` orders the sidebar so the plain demo reads first.\n\nThe one exclusion is **fault injection**: the `control/state.json`-driven simulated hang / forced connection failure is harness machinery, nonsense in a demo project, so it stays only in `test/fixtures/project`. Since examples carry no `control/` fixture, `addon-suite.ts`'s two fault-injection tests `test.skip()` on a `hasControlFixture(projectRoot)` precondition rather than a CI-side filter — one suite, explicit skips in the report, no risk of silently losing coverage if a test gets retitled.\n\n## Deleted two change-detector tests\n\n`test/build-contract.test.ts` and `test/identifier-contract.test.ts` — both asserted a config value or constant against a hardcoded copy of itself, verifying nothing that couldn't already fail elsewhere. Packaging correctness is now verified behaviorally: publint/attw (build-time gates), `test/pack-inventory.test.ts` (a real pack asserting real shipped files), and this PR's `consumer` matrix (a real project installing the tarball and Storybook loading the addon through those exports). Not replaced with anything. Checked the remaining 10 test files for the same pattern — none found, all behavioral.\n\n## Durable context recorded in the repo\n\n- **The published npm version is a name reservation, not a contract** (release plan, next