erified API presence* (the four experimental APIs exist since 10.0.0) while the floor cell proved **API presence ≠ behavioral compatibility** — that's a sharp, reusable lesson for how we set future ranges, and it's exactly the distinction the plan's own \"target, not verified support\" framing was reaching for.\n\nReport which branch, then execute it. Either way we end green *and* honest.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Fix it — real a11y defect, keep the ^10.0.0 range\">\n**Fix it.** Same call as the jsx-runtime defect, and for a stronger reason than \"it unblocks CI\": an icon-only button with no accessible name is **genuinely broken for anyone using a screen reader** on Storybook 10.0.x. Playwright's `getByRole('button', { name: ... })` failed because it queries the accessibility tree exactly as assistive technology does — the test isn't being fussy, it's reporting a real user-facing bug. That reframes this from \"selector mismatch\" to \"a11y defect we'd have shipped.\"\n\nGreat result on the diagnostic, too: the panel rendering identically on 10.0.8 means **the `^10.0.0` support claim survives** — no peer-range revert, no dropping the floor example. The floor cell did its job twice now: challenged the claim, then vindicated it while surfacing a different real bug.\n\n**Fix approach — don't depend on the host component's prop translation at all:**\n- Keep the existing `ariaLabel` prop (harmless, and it satisfies that Storybook 11 deprecation warning you saw about it becoming mandatory).\n- **Add a visually-hidden text child** as the actual accessible name. Content-derived names work in every version of `storybook/internal/components`' `Button` regardless of how it handles props — that's what makes it robust rather than version-lucky. Use the styling approach already present in the codebase if there's a visually-hidden helper; otherwise the standard clip pattern.\n- Optionally a `title` for hover affordance, but don't